- 2026-11-23: Feast master (Episode 1 restart): the two maids came out in Seo-yeon's exact clothes (cream jacket, pink ties, green skirt) because her sheet was the only woman's costume reference and the prompt said only 'two maids'. The user caught it. Rule: every extra or background person gets a written costume that is clearly different from every principal in the frame (servants in plain undyed or grey cotton), and check that no two characters share a costume before passing a master.
- 2026-11-23: 2026-11-23: K18 (Episode 1, 4d two-shot): I passed a keyframe where Do-yun's body faced right but his face turned back toward the camera, away from the hoist he is meant to be looking at; the user had to point it out. Rule: for every keyframe, check each face's direction against the scene map: name the target, find it in the picture (or its screen side), and confirm the nose and eyes point at it. 'Head turned' is not a pass. Write eyelines as profile direction ('face in side profile, nose pointing at the RIGHT edge, toward the hoist').
- 2026-11-23: 2026-11-23: K18 v6 (Episode 1) gave Do-yun a second folded paper in his free hand, though the prompt named one paper in his RIGHT hand. Rule: every keyframe prompt states the count AND the empty hand ('ONE folded paper, in his RIGHT hand; his LEFT hand is empty'), and every keyframe is checked zoomed in on both hands of every person before it is shown or used.
- 2026-11-23: 2026-11-23: Reverse keyframes K11R/K12R (Episode 1): every unnamed gentleman came out with Tae-seok's face (his sheet was the only male face reference), and everyone stared into the lens because the target (Seo-yeon) stood where the camera was. Rule: give every extra a written face clearly different from the principals (age, beard, build); when the eye target stands at the camera, write the eyes passing just beside the lens on a stated side, never 'at the girl'.
- 2026-11-23: 2026-11-23: Reverse shots K11R/K12R looked toward screen RIGHT, the same side as Seo-yeon in K11, breaking the axis; my prompt said 'left of the camera' without naming the picture side. Rule: for any reverse, open the neighbouring shot first, note which screen side its speaker looks to, and write the reverse eyeline as the OPPOSITE screen side in picture terms ('toward the LEFT side of the picture'), then check both side by side.
- 2026-11-23: Scene 3 (23 Nov 2026): the reverse keyframes K11R and K12R still had the guests looking up toward screen RIGHT, the same fault the user flagged before, and I passed them twice (keyframe fix, then clip check) by reading my own prompt wording instead of the faces. Seo-yeon in K11 looks screen RIGHT, so the listeners must look screen LEFT. Also the script's knee strike (a Korean gesture of approval) came out as waving hands and means nothing to an English viewer. Rule: check every reverse eyeline by putting the keyframe beside the shot it answers and judging each nose direction in the picture, never from the prompt text; flag culture-specific gestures to the user before spending on them.
